  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;TARROEQUAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tarroequan is a location mentioned in the context of language comparisons and historical records."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TAIVOAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Taivoan is a location and also a community, known for its dialects and historical significance."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANNACANNAVO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cannacannavo is a location where certain linguistic activities took place, as documented in historical records."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALOELAVAOS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aloelavaos refers to a specific event or activity involving translations in Sinccans, Tarrocquans, and Tevorangs."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DE DAGREGISTERS VAN HET KASTEEL ZEELANDIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DOCUMENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"De Dagregisters van het Kasteel Zeelandia is a document that contains historical records and linguistic observations."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIRAYA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Siraya is an Austronesian language spoken in southwestern Taiwan, historically used as a form of currency and significant in trade and transactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TAIVOAN (TEVORANGH DIALECT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Taivoan (Tevorangh dialect) is a dialect of the Taivoan language, part of the Siraya language family."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MAKATAO (KANAPO DIALECT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Makatao (Kanapo dialect) is another dialect of the Siraya language, distinct from Taivoan."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTO-AUSTRONESIAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proto-Austronesian is the reconstructed ancestor of Austronesian languages, used for comparative linguistic analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Li is a researcher who attempted to classify the relationships among the three Austronesian languages based on linguistic observations."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TAIOUWANG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Taiouwang is an alternative name for Taiwan, derived from the Siraya language."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QAUQAUT TAOKAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Qauqaut Taokas is a term referring to a group of ethnic minorities in Taiwan, including Waishengren, Mongolians, Tibetans, Manchus, Uyghurs, and others."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INDIGENOUS AREAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Indigenous Areas refer to regions inhabited by indigenous peoples in Taiwan, including Siraya and other Austronesian-speaking communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIRAYA (ANCIENT TAIWANESE CURRENCY)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Siraya is an ancient Taiwanese currency used in trade and transactions among indigenous communities, often made from shells or other natural materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PORT IN SIRAYA AREA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The port in the Siraya area refers to a specific location where the port was annexed by the Qing Dynasty in 1683, marking the beginning of a process of gradual acculturation."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QING DYNASTY CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Qing Dynasty China is a historical organization that annexed the port in the Siraya area in 1683, leading to a process of gradual acculturation."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACCULTURATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acculturation is a process where cultural traits are exchanged between groups, leading to changes in social practices and beliefs."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;20TH CENTURY SIRAYA KONGANA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"20th century Siraya Kongana is a specific event or period in the history of the Siraya people, possibly related to linguistic and cultural developments."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIRAIYA SASAAT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Siraiya sasaat is a term used in the Siraya language, possibly referring to a specific linguistic feature or structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DUHA TURU TAPAT TU-RIMA TU-NUM PITU PIPA KUDA KETENG SIRAYA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Duha turu tapat tu-rima tu-num pitu pipa kuda keteng Siraya is a phrase in the Siraya language, possibly describing a linguistic feature or structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOATAO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Moatao is a language that was once considered part of the Siraya language but is now classified as an independent indigenous language."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUANNSAMNA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LANGUAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Suannsamna is a dialect of the Taivoan language, part of the Siraya language family."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TAIBOWAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Taibowan is a location associated with the Taivoan community."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DAZHUANG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dazhuang is a location associated with the Taivoan community."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAISHENGREN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Waishengren refers to immigrants and expatriates from mainland China who settled in Taiwan after the end of the Chinese Civil War."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BURMESE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
